---
/**
 * Who decides whether the document is dark, and when it decides.
 *
 * **This is the only thing that touches the `.dark` class.** It used to be two
 * things — this script and the React toggle's mount effect — and that was the
 * bug rather than an untidiness: the control set the class from its own initial
 * state before correcting itself, a MutationObserver wrote every class change
 * back to `localStorage`, and so a visitor who never opened the menu had a
 * concrete `"light"` or `"dark"` recorded for them on their first load. The site
 * then read that on every later visit and never asked the system again. It
 * persisted the *outcome* where it meant to persist the *choice*.
 *
 * So the split is by concern and not by file size: this owns the document, and
 * `ThemeToggle.astro` owns the menu and holds no state of its own.
 *
 * ## Why it is `is:inline`, and why it is in `<head>`
 *
 * `is:inline` keeps it out of the bundle so it runs during parse rather than
 * after it, and `<head>` is what makes "during parse" early enough to matter.
 * It was in the footer before, which is the end of `<body>` — the class only
 * settled once the whole page had been read, so a dark-scheme visitor could see
 * a light page first. That flash is also what made the old control's
 * set-then-correct dance invisible enough to survive.
 *
 * `src/pages/index.astro` depends on the ordering from the other side: its
 * module mirrors this class onto the published background, and modules run after
 * parsing. Moving this earlier only widens that margin.
 *
 * ## The stored value is a choice, not a scheme
 *
 * `theme-choice` holds `"light"`, `"dark"` or `"system"`, and absent means
 * system — so the default is to follow the OS, and to keep following it. The
 * old `theme` key is removed rather than read: it cannot distinguish a real
 * click from the write the page made on the visitor's behalf, so honouring it
 * would leave everyone who had ever visited pinned to whatever their OS
 * happened to say that day. Clearing it costs a deliberate chooser one click
 * and is the only way the fix reaches anybody.
 */
---

<script is:inline>
  ;(() => {
    const KEY = "theme-choice"
    const media = window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)")

    // Every access is guarded. `localStorage` is not merely empty but *throws*
    // on access in a blocked-cookies profile and in some embedded webviews, and
    // an exception here would leave the document on whatever the stylesheet
    // defaults to with no toggle wired up at all.
    const read = () => {
      try {
        return localStorage.getItem(KEY)
      } catch {
        return null
      }
    }

    const write = (choice) => {
      try {
        localStorage.setItem(KEY, choice)
      } catch {
        /* A session-only choice is a better outcome than a broken page. */
      }
    }

    // Re-reads the choice every time rather than closing over it, which is what
    // lets one function serve both the first paint and every later change.
    const apply = () => {
      const choice = read() ?? "system"
      const dark = choice === "dark" || (choice === "system" && media.matches)
      document.documentElement.classList.toggle("dark", dark)
    }

    try {
      localStorage.removeItem("theme")
    } catch {
      /* Nothing to migrate if storage is unavailable. */
    }

    apply()

    // Live, not once at load. Reading `prefers-color-scheme` a single time is
    // how a session that outlives sunset ends up on the morning's scheme, and it
    // is the whole of what was asked for. `apply` consults the stored choice, so
    // an explicit light or dark ignores this and the two cannot fight.
    media.addEventListener("change", apply)

    // The control's one lever, deliberately narrow: it says which choice was
    // made and this decides what that means. Shaped like `window.showcase` in
    // `src/experiments/gallery/embed.ts`, which solved the same problem for the
    // background and is where the pattern is explained at length.
    window.theme = {
      choice: () => read() ?? "system",
      set: (choice) => {
        write(choice)
        apply()
      },
    }
  })()
</script>
